Auto Ticket Classification using Flow Designer
5.Project Development Phase
5.1 Flow Creation
A new flow is created in Flow Designer.

The flow contains:

Trigger → Conditions → Classification → Assignment → Update

Step 1: Create the Trigger
Configure the trigger to run when a ticket is created.

Example:

Trigger: Record Created
Table: Incident/Ticket

Step 2: Retrieve Ticket Information
The flow reads fields such as:

Short description

Description

Category

Priority

Impact

Urgency

Step 3: Apply Classification Conditions
The flow evaluates the ticket information.

Example:

IF Short Description contains "password"
    Category = Access
    Assignment Group = Service Desk

ELSE IF Short Description contains "laptop"
    Category = Hardware
    Assignment Group = Hardware Support

ELSE IF Short Description contains "network"
    Category = Network
    Assignment Group = Network Support

ELSE IF Short Description contains "application"
    Category = Software
    Assignment Group = Application Support

ELSE
    Category = General
    Assignment Group = Service Desk

Step 4: Update the Ticket
After classification, the flow updates the ticket record.

Possible fields include:

Category

Subcategory

Assignment group

Priority

Work notes

Step 5: Save and Activate the Flow
After configuration, the flow is tested and activated.

5.2 Example
Suppose a user creates a ticket:

Short Description:
"Unable to connect to office Wi-Fi"

The flow identifies the keyword Wi-Fi.

The ticket can then be automatically classified as:

Category: Network Issue

Assignment Group: Network Support

Work Note: Automatically classified by Flow Designer.

This eliminates the need for manual initial classification.

